<body><div class="shell">
<header class="top"><a class="brand" href="/">Sam Connelly</a><nav class="nav" aria-label="Main navigation"><a href="/">Home</a><a href="/work">Work</a><a href="/reading">Reading</a><a href="/notes">Notes</a></nav></header>
<main>
<section class="project-hero"><div class="kicker">Personal project / In development</div><h1>AI Coaching Staff<span style="color:#79977a">.</span></h1><p>What if professional development worked more like football? Watch the film. Get feedback from a coaching staff. Practice a specific skill. Come back better prepared for the next game.</p><div class="project-meta">Python · Structured AI workflows · Local-first · Evidence-based feedback</div></section>
<section class="project-section"><div class="section-label">The playbook / 001</div><h2>Every workday creates game film. Most of us never watch it.</h2><p>Work artifacts become film. The staff studies the evidence, identifies what matters, and turns it into a focused practice plan.</p><figure class="visual playbook"><div class="figure-label">From film to practice</div><img src="/images/coaching-playbook.svg" alt="Football playbook showing work film moving through analysis, specialist coaching, head coach, drills, and player card."/></figure></section>
<section class="project-section"><div class="section-label">The idea</div><h2>Build a facility, not another chatbot</h2><p class="lead">Football teams don't rely on a single pep talk after the game. They study tape, have position coaches review specific decisions, and turn those lessons into practice. I'm applying that model to professional work.</p><p>Meeting notes, technical decisions, and project artifacts become <strong>film</strong>. Each coach looks at a different part of the evidence. The Head Coach decides what to focus on next—not just a long list of generic advice.</p></section>
<section class="project-section"><div class="section-label">Meet the staff / 05</div><h2>One player. Five coaches. One development plan.</h2><p>The staff separates evidence gathering, specialist judgment, and development priorities. Here's the roster.</p><article class="roster-head"><div><div class="number">HEAD COACH / 05</div><h3>Head Coach</h3><div class="role">The final call</div></div><p>Synthesizes the specialist coaches' perspectives, chooses the most important corrections and development priorities, and assigns specific drills.</p></article><div class="roster">{assistants.map((coach) => <article class="coach"><div class="number">STAFF / {coach.number}</div><h3>{coach.name}</h3><div class="role">{coach.role}</div><p>{coach.detail}</p></article>)}</div><p class="player-note">The player is me. The Player Card tracks longer-term progress across skills.</p></section>
<section class="project-section"><div class="section-label">Inside the film room</div><h2>From game tape to a practice plan</h2><p>A fictional example of the kind of specific, evidence-backed feedback the system is designed to produce.</p><figure class="visual"><img src="/images/coaching-scouting-report.svg" alt="Fictional scouting report showing KEEP, CORRECT, DEVELOP and this week's practice drill." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Illustrative scouting report. All observations and situations are fictional.</figcaption></figure><div class="scouting-keys"><div><h3>KEEP</h3><p>Reinforce effective habits.</p></div><div><h3>CORRECT</h3><p>Identify the decision to change.</p></div><div><h3>DEVELOP</h3><p>Choose the skill to improve.</p></div><div><h3>DRILL</h3><p>Practice it in real work.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="project-section"><div class="section-label">The facility</div><h2>A home base for continuous improvement</h2><p>The longer-term vision is a private workspace: the Locker Room for current priorities, the Film Room for past sessions, and the Player Card for development over time.</p><figure class="visual"><img src="/images/coaching-facility-board.svg" alt="Conceptual dashboard showing Locker Room, Film Room and Player Card." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Conceptual interface—not a screenshot of the working application. Building the actual Facility UI is the next step.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="project-section"><div class="section-label">Progress report</div><h2>What's built—and what's next</h2><p>The foundation includes a working Python coaching pipeline, structured film and feedback records, a 40-skill registry, and read-only queries for recent sessions, film details, player-card data, and skill history.</p><div class="chalk-note"><strong>Next on the practice schedule:</strong> Build the real Facility interface, make it easier to revisit coaching, and capture recurring technical concepts and business questions from work.</div><p>The challenge is making feedback useful week after week. The goal is a repeatable practice system, not a one-off AI report.</p><div class="note">This is a public project write-up, not a live demo. Visuals use fictional examples and concepts; private client work and coaching records are not published.</div></section>
